## The published model {#the-published-model .smaller}

::: {.small}
- "citizens (Level 1; N: 390,373) are sampled within 16 EB waves (Level 2) that repeatedly took place within the 27 EU countries (Level 3)" [@Teney2016DoesEUEconomic, p. 623]
- The score of each respondent on sex, age, education, occupation and the type of community, with the national rates of growth and unemployment, and random intercepts for countries and for the country-waves of the surveys
- Model 1 of the paper adds interactions of the two rates with education, so its unemployment coefficient, −0.00340 in Table 3, is the slope of the lowest education band
- On the rebuilt respondents the course refit gives −0.00444 for the lowest band (t = −5.74 on 554.1 Satterthwaite df), and without the interactions −0.00601 (t = −8.39 on 403.9 df), the mark "OA design" of the class chart at r = −0.385
:::

::: {.src}
companion/multilevel.qmd, section 5; companion/_model-outputs/reference_refits.csv.
:::

::: notes
The OA describes the structure in one sentence: citizens sampled within sixteen waves within twenty-seven countries. The model regresses the framing score of each respondent on sex, age, education, occupation and the type of community, with the national rates of growth and unemployment, and with random intercepts for the countries and for the waves in which the respondents were surveyed. Model one of the paper also carries interactions of the two rates with education, so the unemployment coefficient that it prints is the slope for the lowest education band, not an average over the bands. The course refitted the design on the rebuilt respondents both ways. Without the interactions, the slope is the average over the bands, and that refit is the mark labelled OA design on the class chart, so that it answers the same question as the five reanalyses. Both refits leave out the respondents who named nothing, as the code of the OA did, which is why the slope without the interactions is steeper than the one in part three, where those respondents score zero.
:::

