::: notes
Here the course panel and the model of the OA disagree, and the reason lies in the years. The published design has random intercepts for countries and for the waves within them, but nothing for the years, so nothing in it removes a shock that hit every country at once. The fits in the table use the country-years as the middle level, in place of the waves, so the row of the published design differs a little from the slope of part three. 2009 brought such a shock: unemployment rose everywhere and framing moved everywhere, for reasons that include unemployment but are not limited to it. Add a random intercept for each year, or a dummy, and the published estimate on the respondents comes close to the two-way slope of the panel. On the libertarian scale the published design finds a clear positive association, the panel finds almost none, and a term for the years removes the association from the respondents as well. [advance] When a result appears under one defensible design and vanishes under another, that is the strongest argument for fixing the design before any model is fitted.
:::

